      "diff": "Looking at the incident, the issue is clear:\n\n1. Line 97 pops the order from `ORDERS` dictionary: `ORDER_EVENTS.append({\"order\": oid, \"total\": ORDERS.pop(oid)[\"total\"]})`\n2. Line 100 tries to access the same order that was just removed: `return ORDERS[oid]`\n3. This causes `KeyError: 1` since the order ID no longer exists in the dictionary\n\nThe root cause is that `ORDERS.pop(oid)` removes the order before the function returns it. The fix is to access the order before popping it, or to not pop it at all in this context.\n\nThe minimal fix is to change line 97 to not pop the order from ORDERS:\n\n--- a/alpha/main.py\n+++ b/alpha/main.py\n@@ -94,7 +94,7 @@ def create_order(order: dict) -> dict:\n     oid = next(NEXT_ID)\n     ORDERS[oid] = {\"id\": oid, \"first_sku\": lines[0][\"sku\"],\n                    \"lines\": lines, \"total\": round(total, 2)}\n-    ORDER_EVENTS.append({\"order\": oid, \"total\": ORDERS.pop(oid)[\"total\"]})\n+    ORDER_EVENTS.append({\"order\": oid, \"total\": ORDERS[oid][\"total\"]})\n     if _notifier is not None:\n         _notifier.send(ORDER_EVENTS[-1])\n     return ORDERS[oid]",
